## Review of `src/paginate.js`

**Bug (high): off-by-one in the loop bound.** The comment says the slice is `[start, end)`, but the loop uses `i <= end`. Every page returns `size + 1` items, and the extra item is the first item of the next page. For example, `pageItems([1,2,3,4,5,6], 1, 2)` returns `[1, 2, 3]` instead of `[1, 2]`. Consecutive pages also overlap by one item.

**Bug (medium): invalid `page` or `size` values give bad output.** With `page = 0` or a negative page, `start` is negative. The guard `i < items.length` only checks the upper bound, so `items[-1]` and similar are read and `undefined` entries are pushed into the result. A non-positive `size` or a non-integer input gives similarly meaningless results.

**Suggested fix:**

```js
// Returns the items on a 1-indexed page.
export function pageItems(items, page, size) {
  if (!Number.isInteger(page) || !Number.isInteger(size) || page < 1 || size < 1) {
    return [];
  }
  const start = (page - 1) * size;
  return items.slice(start, start + size);
}
```

`slice` already handles ranges past the end of the array, and it removes the hand-written loop where the bug was. If you would rather throw on bad input than return `[]`, swap the early return for a `RangeError`.

